WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.150
- [Instructor] In the last video

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:03.230
we discussed personal rights,

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.970
all the rights that citizens
of the United States have

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.845
to control their own bodies and minds.

00:00:08.845 --> 00:00:12.260
In this video, we're going to
talk about political rights,

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.690
which are the rights of citizens

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:17.960
to participate in the actions
associated with governance.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.820
So, what are some of these rights?

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.744
First, there's freedom of association.

00:00:22.744 --> 00:00:25.460
This means that you can
join a political party

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.290
or a special interest
group that represents you.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.670
You're not obligated to
belong to a particular party

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:33.020
backed by the government,

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.100
and you can't be discriminated against

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.120
for belonging to a political organization.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.040
There are the first amendment
rights to freedom of speech,

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.170
press, assembly, and petition.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.640
This means that you can
share political ideas,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.100
print political ideas, assemble
for political purposes,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.140
like a conference or a protest march,

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.510
and ask the government to correct a wrong

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.570
without fear of punishment.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.760
One of the most important political rights

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.630
is the right to vote.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.890
Citizens may vote as they choose

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.215
according to the laws of their state.

00:01:10.215 --> 00:01:13.330
There's also the right
to run for public office.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:16.990
Anyone who meets the criteria
specified by the Constitution

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.480
and their state or municipality

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.890
may run for elected office
without government interference.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.547
Now, you may be asking,
"Wait, what's the difference

00:01:26.547 --> 00:01:29.272
"between political
rights and civil rights?"

00:01:29.272 --> 00:01:32.580
Well, civil rights
overlap with these rights,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.940
as well as with personal rights,

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.240
because civil rights protect people

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.520
who belong to marginalized
groups from discrimination.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.520
So, if a society permits some
people to vote but not others,

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.200
based on their race, sex,
religion, or other characteristic,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.660
that society is infringing

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.840
on the civil rights of those citizens

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.600
by denying them their political rights.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.710
So, let's take a look at a few scenarios

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.930
to practice our understanding
of political rights.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:06.480
I'll give you a scenario and
you see if you can identify

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.260
which political rate is
being exercised or violated.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.640
First, Parvati's best friend, Natalie,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.330
is running for city council.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:17.730
She wants to show her support,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.370
so, she checks Natalie's
name on the election ballot.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.373
Which right did Parvati exercise?

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.222
If you said the right
to vote, you're correct.

00:02:30.222 --> 00:02:31.500
All right, next up.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.160
Peih-Gee posts on her social
media encouraging people

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.400
to vote for the Democratic
candidate for governor.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.800
An admissions officer

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.750
at the public university Peih-Gee got into

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.310
is the brother of the
Republican candidate.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.610
He rescinds Peih-Gee's offer of admission

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.410
based on her support for
the Democratic candidate.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.780
Which political right did the
admissions officer violate

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:55.053
in this scenario?

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.740
This is a violation of Peih-Gee's right

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.640
to political freedom of speech.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:05.860
Okay, last one.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.010
Spencer has been an avid
hunter since he was a kid

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:12.610
and is interested in joining
the National Rifle Association.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.230
His boss, Charles, doesn't like guns,

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:18.230
and doesn't want Spencer to join the NRA.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:21.650
If Charles tries to prohibit
Spencer from joining the NRA,

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.363
which of Spencer's rights
would he be violating?

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.260
He would be violating Spencer's right

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.470
to freedom of association,

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.030
which means that he can join groups

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:34.060
without fear of reprisal.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.330
Okay, that's all for now.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:38.460
Which do you think is the
most important political right

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:39.833
that citizens have?

